     "result_text_tail": "ing.** I treated FY2026 as the first year of the plan, because the pack's lender minimum, capital window and draft plan all do. If FY2026 has already ended by the February meeting, the lender slide needs the actual result instead.\n2. **Constant FY2025 prices and volumes.** I assumed price rises cover normal cost inflation. The only headwind I modelled is the 2027 minimum-wage rise, assuming half of it is offset.\n3. **Estimates the pack doesn't support directly.** These are all labelled in the deck:\n   - Cutting promotions loses a third of their sales volume.\n   - Vendor funding recovers half its slide. This assumes the pack's 14-point promotion margin gap is measured after vendor funding, which finance should confirm.\n   - The most labor-intensive quarter of stores holds about 28% of hours.\n   - A second delivery price step adds about $2.5M; it still needs testing.\n   - The 9 stores' numbers are split evenly by store count.\n4. **The 3 stores whose leases run past 2027** go to a board vote in Q2 FY2027, after two quarters on the new playbook, rather than now. Exiting them costs $7.5M, paid back in about 3.4 years.\n5. **One-time costs other than that $7.5M** (closing the 6 stores, private-label development, shrink tools) aren't in the pack, so I left them to finance. That leaves $17.5M of the $25M allowance.\n\nThe appendix slides show how each number was sized and list what to confirm before the plan is locked.\n\nFiles are in ./deliverable:\n- Harlow_margin_plan_board_Feb2027.pptx"
    }
